<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # We All Do Chores – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #333; margin-top: 5px; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 20px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 5th Grade (CEFR: A1.3)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Helping Around the House</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> We All Do Chores.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Describe favorite hobbies or activities briefly. Interactive: Exchange information about hobbies with peers.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can describe a favorite household chore or activity using simple sentences (e.g., "I like cooking with my mom." "My favorite chore is watering the plants."). Can have a simple discussion by asking and answering questions about chores and activities they do at home (e.g., "Do you help clean the house?" "Yes, I clean my room." "What chore do you like?" "I like doing the dishes."). Can explain how they do simple chores at home using basic sentences (e.g., "I sweep and mop the floor every day." "I wash the dishes and then dry them.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 10px; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays flashcards of various household chores (e.g., wash the dishes, sweep the floor, cook, water the plants, tidy up the room). Students stand up. The teacher calls out a chore, and students perform a simple action for that chore (e.g., pretend to sweep, wash hands for dishes). Teacher asks: "What chore is this?" and students respond chorally, "It's [washing the dishes]!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences about chores using simple present and frequency adverbs. For example, pointing to a "wash the dishes" flashcard, the teacher says: "I wash the dishes every day." (Pronounce /w/ clearly). Then, pointing to "sweep the floor," the teacher says: "I sweep the floor often." (Pronounce /s/ clearly). The teacher also models asking and answering "What do you do at home?" and "I help my mom [clean the house]." The teacher draws attention to the closed syllable in "cook" and the open syllable in "do" by clapping out syllables.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do I wash the dishes sometimes or every day?" (Expected answer: "Every day!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'sweep' a verb or a noun?" (Expected answer: "A verb!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Helping Around the House")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short dialogue between two children, "Leo and Mia," discussing their household chores. The dialogue is written on a large chart or projected, with key vocabulary (e.g., *wash, clean, sweep, cook, help, organize, dust, dishes, floor, kitchen*) and grammar structures (present simple, WH-questions, frequency adverbs like "every day," "often") highlighted. Example dialogue:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: none; padding-left: 0; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Leo:</b> "Hi Mia! What do you do at home to help?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mia:</b> "Hi Leo! I often help my mom. I wash the dishes every day."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Leo:</b> "Wow! What else do you do?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Mia:</b> "I also sweep the floor in the living room. My brother waters the plants."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Leo:</b> "That's great! I clean my bedroom and organize my clothes."</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Sociolinguistic: Discussing routines; Describing daily tasks and house chores according to context.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist questions (oral):</b> "What are Leo and Mia talking about?" "Do they help at home?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (oral):</b> "Mia washes the dishes every day. True or False?" "Leo waters the plants. True or False?" (Students say "True" or "False").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Present simple for routines (I wash, He washes), WH-questions (What do you do?), Frequency adverbs (every day, often, usually). Vocabulary: dishes, house, chores, broom, floor, kitchen, family, wash, clean, sweep, cook, help, organize, dust, water.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: decimal; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Oral Cloze:</b> The teacher reads sentences about chores aloud, pausing for students to complete them with the correct word. Example: "I _______ the dishes every day." (Students say: "wash!"). "My sister _______ the floor." (Students say: "sweeps!"). "What _______ you do?" (Students say: "do!"). This reinforces pronunciation of /w/ and /s/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Substitution Drill (Pair Work):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides sentence frames and a list of vocabulary words. Students take turns saying sentences, substituting words.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Frame 1: "I _______ the _______ [every day/often]." (e.g., wash the dishes, clean my bedroom, water the plants, sweep the floor).</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Frame 2: "My [mom/dad/brother/sister] _______ the _______." (e.g., cooks the food, dusts the table, folds the laundry).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Guided Pair Interaction - Q&A:</b> Students stay in pairs. The teacher provides question prompts and answer starters on the board or a handout. Students ask and answer questions about chores.
                        <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Question Prompt: "What do you do at home?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Answer Starter: "I usually _______ and _______." or "I help my _______ to _______."</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Question Prompt: "What chore do you like?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Answer Starter: "I like _______ because it's _______." (e.g., tidy, important).</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Helping Around the House")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: "My Family's Chores Interview"</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in pairs. Each student receives a simple "interview card" with prompts to ask their partner about their household chores. The goal is to find out what chores their partner and their family members do.</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Instructions:</b></li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Student A interviews Student B, then they switch roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students use the target grammar (Present Simple, WH-questions) and vocabulary from the theme "We All Do Chores."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interview Prompts (on cards):</b></li>
                        <ul style="list-style-type: circle; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What do you do at home to help?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How often do you [wash the dishes/clean your room/water the plants]?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What chore does your mom/dad/brother/sister do?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do you like to [sweep the floor/cook]?"</li>
                        </ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Expected output:</b> Students will engage in simple dialogues like: "What do you do at home?" "I clean my room every day." "How often do you wash the dishes?" "I wash them often." "What chore does your mom do?" "My mom cooks food."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during "My Family's Chores Interview." The teacher circulates and notes participation, use of target language, and pronunciation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform the "My Family's Chores Interview" with their partner.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /w/ (wash) and /s/ (sweep).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., dishes, floor, clean, wash, help).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Asks and answers WH-questions about chores (What do you do? How often?).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses Present Simple correctly in simple sentences (I wash, He cleans).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaborates with partner to complete the interview.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on a small slip of paper before leaving:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new chore I learned to talk about today is: _______."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can describe _______ chores I do at home." (Students write a number: 1, 2, 3...)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me when talking about chores today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult, and what can I practice more at home?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The paired interview activity was highly engaging, and most students successfully used present simple and target vocabulary. The pronunciation focus on /w/ and /s/ was effective during modeling, but some students still struggled to consistently differentiate. Adjustments needed: Provide more explicit drills for problematic sounds before main speaking tasks. Scaffold more sentence starters for lower-level students during production to boost confidence. Review common errors during feedback.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This speaking lesson has prepared students to confidently discuss their chores. In Lesson 4, students will use this oral practice to describe their daily chores in writing, focusing on sentence structure and paragraph organization.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>